{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition","id":1486014,"title":"Is Assamite Sorcery the weakest Blood Magic Tradition?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-07-07T17:01:20-07:00","id":1486014,"title":"Is Assamite Sorcery the weakest Blood Magic Tradition?","content":"IMHO Assamite Sorcery has some extremely useless rituals. Dont get me wrong, rituals like Horoscope and Astral Portent are pretty neat, but they're in a minority. Now yes, I know most of the rituals are there to communicate between the Assamites at Alamut with the other Assamites around the world, but at this point they might as well adopt Hactivist Thaumathurgy, an Anarch Sorcry Wave that is used by the Digital Draculas. It's just a lot more practical, and gives more bang for your buck.<br /> <br /> Now some of the unique paths look pretty cool, but feel like they would be very situational. Also, the name of the powers for Blessings of The Great Dark Mother makes no sense either, they chose perhaps the most inapropriate names. Could they honestly not think of anything different?<br /> <br /> What do you think?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":27313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","user":"Hello","date":". 07-08-2022, 08:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-07T22:29:27-07:00","id":1486028,"title":"","content":"Actually it is quite fitting in to the Lore<br /> Don't forget, according Assamite CB, approximately two centuries after Haqim established the Judges the Baali started to appear in the Second City and they got simpathizers witihin various vampire families. Saulot claimed his holy warriors were capable of piercing and culling such demonic sorcery, Haqim had little trus left for the cousin who had all but forced him into the position of law-keeper. Haqim left the city, and when he returned three years later, he had only three followers with him. The first publicly acknowledged blood magicans of the Second City.<br /> Okay, the first publicly acknowledged blood magicans not necessarily means they were the actual first blood magicans, but they are pretty old and were among the first<br /> And magical traditions change... slowly. Or actually not at all. The assamite sorcery always were a very specialized branch, not necessarily practical per se, but specialized. To upkeep the LAW, to help the Judges, to combat the Baali.<br /> Times changed... the Assamite Sorcery? Not <i>that</i> much. Never had to. And later the whole Clan became the extension of th Web of Knives and the Viziers became a minority<br /> Awakening of the Steel, Hunter's Winds, Whispers of the Heavens, Keepers Way, Hand of the Magi (Lure of Flames), Life's Water (Path of Blood), Path of the Ailing Jackal (Path of the Father's Vengeance), Path of Warding, Alchemy... thesre are all fitting and to be honest quite powerfull paths<br /> <br /> Lot of people thinks Dur-An-Ki = Assamite Sorcery.<br /> This is not the case!<br /> Dur-An-Ki is a form of magic that originated in ancient Mesopotamia that Assamite Sorcerers have incorporated into their own practices<br /> Covenant of Nergal and Evil Eye have Baali roots for example and I doubt you find any Vizier using these paths. Study? Sure. But use? Press X to doubt<br /> Other Dur-An-Ki paths have roots in India mostly with their own traditons and belief system<br /> <br /> As for Blessings of the Great Dark Mother?<br /> It a fitting Path for the Viziers<br /> And the name is fitting too... Haqim never was the biggest fan of Caine (actually, if you take the Assamite story, they are enemies) and Lilith, the Dark Mother, similarly enemy of Caine?<br /> Its not that hard to put together two and two in my humble oppinion<br /> <br /> Assamire sorcery is an ancient form of blood magic, specialized and developed for a reason... not really changed in the past ~ 8000 years or so, never had to, and the wolrd moved on, now schools, new traditions, but the tradtitional (and a bit opressed Viziers) worked with what they had<br /> Still, its paths are quite powerfull in their own right and very flavorfull, perfect for the Clan as a whole<br /> Anyhow this is only my two cents, but I don't think it is &quot;weak&quot; or the &quot;weakest&quot;... just different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-07T23:27:51-07:00","id":1486029,"title":"","content":"It's supposed to include at least half of the core book's &quot;Tremere&quot; rituals and paths.<br /> <br /> But if we want to talk about story, it's the weakest of the three largest traditions (Tremere Hermetic thaumaturgy,FoS Ahku) for both mechanism and logistics.<br /> <br /> Tremere Heremetic Thaumaturgy:<br /> <b>Scope</b>: Hermetic theory is an all encompassing, moderate, idea: All religions are part true, so put aside your differences, come together and let's do magic. If it exists, magic for it also exists. If some other magical practice can do it, we can do it to. 10/10<br /> <b>Mechanisms</b>: Tremere do really well in standardizing their practice and their members are relatively uniform which is really good for teaching new people. 10/10<br /> <b>Structure</b>: The Tremere's entire clan structure revolves around supporting thaumaturges. There's a little brainwashing and blood bonding going on, and anything involving vampires and big egos is going to have a little dysfunction. But honestly? I think this is the best system for vampires. 9/10<br /> <br /> Average: 9.6, will invariably monopolize the magic of the world.<br /> <br /> <b>Settite Akhu</b><br /> Scope: Everything needs to be put in the framework of Set. You are limited to mimicking what you think the God/His allies can do. This would give the FoS a 6/10 but fortunately the FoS are really very liberal with what Set is and who his allies are, so I'd give it a 7.<br /> Mechanism: Getting really, really into roleplaying an egyptian priest is the way to work the magic; it's fun. The LARP might cut into your ritual time, but nothings so gratifying as to work the magic over the god you geek out to. 7/10<br /> Organization: Everyone's bound together by a love of Set. Practicing and sharing the magic with believers is proof of your love of Set: You're in a cult, and that's actually a good thing for you. However FOS don't get together in excellent structures like the Tremere to effectively divide labour, so more of their time is spent on cult micromanaging and politicking, which is probably why magic is an out-of-clan for them. 7/10<br /> <br /> Average: 7/10<br /> <br /> <b>Assamite Dur-An-Ki</b><br /> Scope: Like hermeticism, Dur-An-Ki, also encompases everything, but unlike hermetics, Assamite sorcers do not &quot;meet in the middle&quot;. Dur-an-Ki is too broad. It contains both dirty witches making covenants with spirits and elegant scholars drawing power from the stars: these people aren't likely to get along and see the opposite extreme's practices as bad for their own. I give it a 5/10.<br /> Mechanism: there's no standardization and not much in the way of shared beliefs. Also most books have such mages require drugs or pain or some other extreme state. 3/10.<br /> Structure: No standardization, being dependent/hostage to the other castes, not really having local Assamite sorcerers who can spend time to train neonates means an increased reliance on embracing the rare few who already practice magic, further limiting numbers and muddling the standardization 3/10.<br /> <br /> Average: 3.6. They're failures who rely on natural talent and who are pigeonholed into an undesirable system by an unforgiving caste system. But magic in the WoD isn't about talent, it's about knowledge. There might be some exceptional individuals among their ranks that can go toe to toe with some of the best Tremere has to offer, but the Tremere has more of those.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 07-08-2022, 02:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T08:11:20-07:00","id":1486041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486028#post1486028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually it is quite fitting in to the Lore<br /> <br /> <br /> As for Blessings of the Great Dark Mother?<br /> It a fitting Path for the Viziers<br /> And the name is fitting too... Haqim never was the biggest fan of Caine (actually, if you take the Assamite story, they are enemies) and Lilith, the Dark Mother, similarly enemy of Caine?<br /> Its not that hard to put together two and two in my humble oppinion<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I was actually referring to the names of the individual powers that make up Blessings of the Great Dark Mother. Beyond the first level, the description of each power states that it is named after a daughter of Lucifer and Lilith. I looked up Memo, Sotheq, Kessep, none of them were daughters of Lilith. I dont even think I need to explain why it's extremely innapropriate to name Allah as a daughter of Lilith and Lucifer. This was published in 2016, how did this even get through editing?<br /> <br /> You can find it in <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#3A3A3A\">The Black Hand: A Guide to the Tal\u2019Mahe\u2019Ra, pg 86-87 (In case anybody wants get a better picture of what I am talking about)</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.nodiatis.com/pub/18.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":27313,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","user":"Hello","date":". 07-08-2022, 11:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27313-hello","name":"Hello","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27313_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T12:02:36-07:00","id":1486057,"title":"","content":"Oh I see!<br /> Well true<br /> Kessep (Silver) and Shotheq (Silence), and Nesher (Eagle) were Lucifer and Lilith's sons and Mem (Water), Oreb (Raven), and Laylah (Night) were their daughters. They born in D'hainu, the Garden of Renewal<br /> The Bahari believe these six children in her gardens were slain when Caine and his coterie of Antediluvians attacked her Garden<br /> <br /> It is a bit mistery why the name &quot;corruption&quot; but I think you are correct, its basically a typo that slipped thrugh editing and after V20 basically ended no one bothered to correct it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T12:29:02-07:00","id":1486059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486029#post1486029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's supposed to include at least half of the core book's &quot;Tremere&quot; rituals and paths.<br /> <br /> But if we want to talk about story, it's the weakest of the three largest traditions (Tremere Hermetic thaumaturgy,FoS Ahku) for both mechanism and logistics.</div> </div> </div> </div>I politely disagree with the width of the difference and many details of the conclusion, but not with the general gist of it.<br /> <br /> My own perception of the matter, taking only the lore:<br /> <b>Scope</b>: Tremere Hermeticism is all encompassing, but exists under a rigid and exceedingly complex theoretical framework. While it's useful for the final result, breaking an idea and reassembling it as Thaumaturgy is a lengthy process that shouldn't have a seamless result. Akhu has a solid basis that makes developing new magic much more a matter of intuition and inspiration. Dur-An-Ki has an idea of accepting other magical practices at face value that makes incorporating things from the outside basically plug-and-play.<br /> <b>Mechanisms</b>: Disagree completely here. The standardization is a good thing, but Tremere Hermeticism has a high threshold to start doing stuff. Getting the theory is important, understanding why a ritual works is important. Once you get to it it's easy to get how the stuff your peers do work and study them, but until you figure it out you're on tough waters. Akhu is time and resource consuming, but the fact it relies on religion, again, makes it very intuitive for the practitioner. Dur-An-Ki is a middle ground where you do need to study a whole new thing each time, but you don't need to understand it in a deeper level, the fact that it work is good enough, and itt does make for an easier time learning whatever the student already had some understanding of, as they don't have to unlearn and learn again under a new magical paradigm.<br /> <b>Structure</b>: Here I mostly agree. The Tremere simply has a far better clan structure to support sorcerers. Even if local Tremere aren't the greatest practitioners (the clan does need people doing other things), they have a Chantry with access to their extensive support network to help new sorcerers, and standardization goes a long way into making the material accessible across the board. But I wouldn't so completely dismiss the Banu Haqim here, the sorcerers usually have contact with each other and some gathering places, and while it isn't easy for them to share magic, it is to find tutors in the specifics you want, something that isn't always easy on the Tremere side because specific knowledge is something they're encouraged to hoard and dispute over, while the Haqim Sorcerers don't have much to lose spreading any particular magic in the clan.<br /> <br /> With this, I completely agree that the Tremere as a clan are certainly superior, but I wouldn't dismiss the Sorcerers as failures at all. The ultimate advantage of the Tremere is actually something the game doesn't even have a lot of rules on, rituals with multiple participants. Every time on the lore the Tremere outdone everyone else, it was this kind of ritual, which they can organize and execute in a scale no one else can compete with.<br /> <br /> But from a mechanics perspective, that is what matters here, I'd say at least in V20 the Sorcerers actually have the strongest magic, not the weakest. Because of two small rules only they have: the possibility for the ST to add Paths to their list of options just 'cause, and the option to learn <i>anything</i> from other magical Disciplines at +50% cost. With this, while their standard options are limited, they're solid, they have an exclusive Path, and they have potential access to anything else even if at extra cost, something the Tremere can't do. That means a Banu Haqim can potentially learn anything under <b>Necromancy</b>, <b>Thaumaturgy</b> and <b>Koldunic</b> in the core book alone, and even anything the ST introduces in the game by any means for any Blood Magic. If you add <i>Rites of the Blood</i>, then they get even more powerful with access to <i>Kalif</i>, as difficulty reduction is one hell of a rare resource in VtM.<br /> <br /> Personally I don't think the rules as they stand now are very good to demonstrate any of the lore differences, and I'm strongly against rules that make any choice for the players objectively inferior, so I tend to be a bit more lenient with access to Paths the player wants. And given that the rules do not require or even suggest to be too strict on which Path pertains to which Discipline, I don't think there should be a lot of practical differences in power as they are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T13:07:50-07:00","id":1486063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486059#post1486059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. Dur-An-Ki has an idea of accepting other magical practices at face value that makes incorporating things from the outside basically plug-and-play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's your own biases. Just because the Assamites have a broad spectrum of beliefs doesn't mean they can just adopt anything that chalenges their personal beliefs. If you can believe in anything, you believe in nothing. Mages and Thaumaturges are far too stubborn to have such a crowd pleasing cop-out.<br /> <br /> <b>Mechanisms</b>: Disagree completely here. The standardization is a good thing, but Tremere Hermeticism has a high threshold to start doing stuff. Getting the theory is important, understanding why a ritual works is important. Once you get to it it's easy to get how the stuff your peers do work and study them, but until you figure it out you're on tough waters. Akhu is time and resource consuming, but the fact it relies on religion, again, makes it very intuitive for the practitioner. Dur-An-Ki is a middle ground where you do need to study a whole new thing each time, <b>but you don't need to understand it in a deeper level</b>, [/quote]<br /> WTF did I just read? Thaumaturges who dedicate their unlives to their practice don't need to understand their practice?<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I wouldn't so completely dismiss the Banu Haqim here, the sorcerers usually have contact with each other and some gathering places, and while it isn't easy for them to share magic, it is to find tutors in the specifics you want, something that isn't always easy on the Tremere side because specific knowledge is something they're encouraged to hoard and dispute over, while the Haqim Sorcerers don't have much to lose spreading any particular magic in the clan. </div> </div> </div>So Assamite sorcerers don't politick like Tremere do? Ehrm... If anything, it'll be worse, because the Tremere can at least agree on the way of doing things, while the Assamites can have a fallout over whether method X,Y or Z is best. I'm also struggling to think where you're going to get a tutor as an assamite? Assamite sorcerers are FEW and the realities of vampire politics means that cities don't generally let vampires come and go like they're EU citizens in Europe, nevermind that the Assamites are a super-militant sect that everyone's wary of.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That means a Banu Haqim can potentially learn anything under <b>Necromancy</b>, <b>Thaumaturgy</b> and <b>Koldunic</b> in the core book alone, and even anything the ST introduces in the game by any means for any Blood Magic. If you add <i>Rites of the Blood</i>, </div> </div> </div>Erhm, if they wrote that they can learn Necromancy and Koldunic sorcery RAW, and I don't think they did, I don't think that's RAI, and I don't think most ST's would allow it.<br /> <br /> [quote]then they get even more powerful with access to <i>Kalif</i>, as difficulty reduction is one hell of a rare resource in VtM.[quote]<br /> Isn't there a library background to make it easier with less sideaffects?<br /> Kaliff's a double edged sword; some of the writing makes it seem like the AsSc depend on it and can't do without, whilst other sections seem to suggest it's a nice bonus resource.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Personally I don't think the rules as they stand now are very good to demonstrate any of the lore differences, and I'm strongly against rules that make any choice for the players objectively inferior, so I tend to be a bit more lenient with access to Paths the player wants. And given that the rules do not require or even suggest to be too strict on which Path pertains to which Discipline, I don't think there should be a lot of practical differences in power as they are. </div> </div> </div>Mechanically I run them the same.<br /> Story wise, there are pros and cons to both sides. Covenants with spirits or cosmic oppertunities are a fun curveball with Assamites.<br /> I'm actually quite lenient with AsSc: Warriors and Viziers <i>can </i>learn a select few powers from sorcerers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T14:49:09-07:00","id":1486071,"title":"","content":"It's a bit outside of rules and lore of the game, but I collect grimoires as a hobby and have a few from the middle east, the classics from Egypt, and multiple hermetic ones. If I were to make an analogy, I would liken the differences to mathematics: the basic language and rules are the same, but the application varies on what you are doing. If Hermeticism is like calculus, middle eastern magic is like algebra.<br /> Formalization isn't something that's exclusive to hermetic magic either, it's more dependent on time than discipline; old spells rely more on theurgic invocations, new spells more on ritual, symbolism, correspondences and established conventions. Eventually all traditions start to borrow heavily from each other when the world started to get smaller.<br /> <br /> Bottom line, if the Assamites and Setites didn't stop developing their magic (and why would they), the scope should be pretty even between them and the Tremere. If something doesn't fit your paradigm, you can always develop an analog that does. I think it's also important to consider how much an occult library can mitigate <i>some</i> of the structural differences- and Mesopotamia and Egypt definitely had a head-start with written records. I would still give the Tremere the overall edge, simply because of the superior internal organization.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-08T18:31:23-07:00","id":1486079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that's your own biases. Just because the Assamites have a broad spectrum of beliefs doesn't mean they can just adopt anything that chalenges their personal beliefs.</div> </div> </div> </div>According to <i>Clanbook: Assamite (Revised)</i><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>pp. 73, paragraphs 2 and 3</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;We should not be ashamed<br /> to acknowledge truth from whatever source it comes to us,<br /> even if it is brought to us by former generations and foreign<br /> peoples. For him who seeks the truth there is nothing of<br /> higher value than truth itself.&quot;<br /> <br /> The sorcerers took this and similar admonitions to<br /> heart, and still pay heed to such advice in the modern<br /> nights. The twin watchwords of Assamite blood magic,<br /> which seem slightly contradictory to many initiates, are<br /> 'discipline' and 'pragmatism'. With so many different ways to<br /> reach the same goal, Assamite studies of blood magic often<br /> guide a student toward whichever methods work best for<br /> him, even going so far as to trade an apprentice among five<br /> or six mentors before an agreeable partnership arises.</div> </div> </div> </div>They don't have a <u><b>clan</b></u>-defined standard belief for magic. Since the beginning their <u><b>clan</b></u> orientation towards magic is about practical use. That's why any tradition can be studied to begin with, because the beliefs of that tradition do not matter <b><u>to the clan</u></b>.<br /> <br /> What a specific Sorcerer can do may be bound by personal beliefs, although those can be syncretic combinations that they rework with their personal theories as they incorporate Paths if it comes to this. It is not a crowd pleasing cop-out, it is a tradition of studying anything related to magic without trying to apply your own systems to it.<br /> <br /> If you can't imagine someone working magic without buying on the full belief system behind it, or an academic institution accepting people of all kinds of beliefs, then that's <b>your</b> bias, because Dur-An-Ki has never been about the clan beliefs and faith, old, new or &quot;newer&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now, there are some distinctions in three later books. <i>Libellus Sanguinis 3</i> introduced the idea of a unifying paradigm of achieving an altered state of mind, which can be done with anything from meditation to consuming <i>Kalif</i>, but it is still subordinate to the lack of any unifying belief to their magic as expressed earlier, and as a Dark Ages supplement should be taken with a grain of salt anyways.<br /> <br /> <i>Blood Sacrifice: the Thaumaturgy Companion</i> introduced, years later, the concept of Dur-An-Ki being based on the use of altered states to commune with spirits and magic coming from the pacts made with those spirits. But while the idea is lengthy described, it is poorly developed. Those pacts have no religious component nor involve service, yet they draw from religions, and they include use of scriptures, and you can invoke a pagan deity and beg for God's permission in the same ritual, and it is generally confuse, even if you can get the intention with some effort.<br /> <br /> Finally, <i>Rites of the Blood</i> tried to unify the clashing propositions and simplify the mess in <i>Blood Sacrifice</i>. It goes in less detail about how their magic is pragmatic first, but still keep to the <i>Clanbook</i> description of it coming from diverse sources without much thought to a unifying theory, then talks about the &quot;<i>Ladder to Heaven&quot;</i>, a <u>new</u> idea that gained momentum to use altered states to reach spirits and learn magic from them, but that's just another tradition being incorporated even if it is very strong by now.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you can believe in anything, you believe in nothing.</div> </div> </div> </div>So says you, not the lore on the clan. This lore, instead, says that if something works, you must first accept that it works and shut the fuck up, your make belief won't stop it from working. If it works, it has some truth, if it has some truth, it can and should be studied by its own merits.<br /> <br /> The Assamites simply do not follow your trope about magic users, no matter how common it is in other parts of the setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">WTF did I just read? Thaumaturges who dedicate their unlives to their practice don't need to understand their practice?</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't said that, stop making straw men of my words.<br /> <br /> You can understand the difference between &quot;studying several approaches to magic to <u><b>develop</b></u> new Paths and rituals&quot; and &quot;Having a <b><u>deep</u></b> understanding just to cast a spell&quot;. I was comparing <b>Dur-An-Ki</b> with frigging <b>Tremere Hermeticism</b>. One of them accepts that every magic that works, works as is. The other that every magic that works must be disassembled and understood under the light of their own unifying theory, to only then be reassembled as <b>Thaumaturgy</b>. Both have to study and understand what they do, but their requirements for mere replication aren't equal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Assamite sorcerers don't politick like Tremere do?</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, you're building straw men from my words, stop.<br /> <br /> Both politick and both have arcane power as a badge of status, but their internal economy is completely different for several reasons. Their caste system and few numbers pretty much force them to cooperate as a means to keep their status among the castes, their goal of breaking their clan-wide curse(s) presses them to work together for an answer to the conundrum, their less hierarchical internal structure (yes, they have a hierarchy, but you won't say that they have something even remotely similar to the Pyramid, will you?) means that to establish the pecking order and benefit from internal status they have to spread knowledge instead of hoard it (as in academia, you raise by being the most copied and referred, not by hoarding).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Assamite sorcerers are FEW and the realities of vampire politics means that cities don't generally let vampires come and go like they're EU citizens in Europe, nevermind that the Assamites are a super-militant sect that everyone's wary of.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not only does the book blatantly says that a student going to 5-6 mentors is common practice, this ignores that no clan or bloodline spreads through the globe by being randomly assigned a territory as if it was a game of <i>Risk</i>. The Sorcerers keep in contact, and unless they're being sent alone to some location for a specific purposes, it is unlikely that one won't be in relatively short distance from another one.<br /> <br /> Also, you know, they can communicate at a distance, like the Tremere ca, too. Trading notes and teaching don't have to be <i>t\u00eate \u00e0 t\u00eate</i> all the time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Erhm, if they wrote that they can learn Necromancy and Koldunic sorcery RAW, and I don't think they did, I don't think that's RAI, and I don't think most ST's would allow it.</div> </div> </div> </div>From the V20 Core:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>pp. 440, under Assamite Sorcery, paragraph 4</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As may be expected, students of Assamite Sorcery<br /> have great difficulty learning the practices of other<br /> blood magic traditions. All experience points costs to<br /> learn other blood magic paths and rituals are increased<br /> by half (round up) for Assamite sorcerers. In addition,<br /> even once the sorcerer has incorporated these lessons<br /> into her repertoire, they are still alien to her. All in-<br /> vocations of a \u201cforeign\u201d path require one extra blood<br /> point and all rituals take triple the normal time and<br /> require one extra success for any desired result.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is taken directly from the entry on their magic from <i>Clanbook: Assamite</i> and this is the only Blood Magic with such an entry of a rule to learn magic from other traditions. If this RAW isn't RAI, I don't know what the hell it could be, and if an ST will allow or not this is not my problem. I would actually make it easier because I think having both higher XP cost and higher requirements in everything else is stupid, but YMMV.<br /> <br /> As for the list of Paths, this is in th same book, same page:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>side box, paragraph 1</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The<br /> following list is not an exhaustive coverage<br /> of everything in the Great Library, but it is a<br /> starting point for players and Storytellers who<br /> wish to determine the capabilities of Assamite<br /> sorcerers.</div> </div> </div> </div>So the ST is actively suggested to expand on the list of available Paths, while the list do not have to include everything it isn't intended to be limited to the examples given.<br /> <br /> You can read both together as meaning that any Path the ST adds is under the increased burden, but <b><u>that</u></b> I definitely do not see as the intended interpretation in any way, it would just be the ST being a jerk.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't there a library background to make it easier with less sideaffects?</div> </div> </div> </div>Mage has a Library Background, but it doesn't have a similar effect (and Mage has this kind of effect from a lot of things). I do have a faint memory of seeing a Vampire book that had Library, but I can't quite remember which book. I'm almost sure it wasn't like the effect of <i>Kalif</i>.<br /> <br /> And the effect isn't a double-edged sword in this sense, what happens is that some sections emphasize a lot its role for the Sorcerers as the greatest boon for their magic. But the rules are quite straightforward, actually, it isn't needed for Paths or Rituals, but it gives a bonus in the form of Difficulty reduction for both for a scene if you manage to get a light trip. The bonus depends on the quality of the drug, which is determined by the Generation of the donor of the blood used to make it, Quality is effective Generation Background, the reduction is half the Quality (rounded up, I guess).<br /> <br /> It is kinda tricky to use and unnecessarily complicated rules-wise, but not by much. Depending on your contacts it wouldn't be hard to get something capable of reducing difficulties by -2 or even -3, as this is within what you can make with a starting character. And if you fail to use it properly the worse that can happen is for you to waste time enjoying yourself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-09T12:00:24-07:00","id":1486108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486079#post1486079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">According to <i>Clanbook: Assamite (Revised)</i></div> </div> </div> </div>...this is... interesting. <br /> But having to go through what can be up to six potential mentors is really a testament of how fractured the sorcerers must be when it comes to beliefs. Imagine it: You embrace a promising childer with a gift, and after weeks of disagreements over style and finding out the way you've dedicated yourself to isn't suitable for your childer you now find yourself obligated to hawk them off to another sorcerer, very likely stationed in another city, and causing a prestation burden every time the child needs to be passed on. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They don't have a <u><b>clan</b></u>-defined standard belief for magic. Since the beginning their <u><b>clan</b></u> orientation towards magic is about practical use. That's why any tradition can be studied to begin with, because the beliefs of that tradition do not matter <b><u>to the clan</u></b>. </div> </div> </div>To the clan, they don't matter. So long as you're not conjuring demons or praising Set, the clan doesn't mind. But as individuals with particularly big egos that use their will to alter reality? Beliefs matter. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you can't imagine someone working magic without buying on the full belief system behind it, or an academic institution accepting people of all kinds of beliefs, then that's <b>your</b> bias, because Dur-An-Ki has never been about the clan beliefs and faith, old, new or &quot;newer&quot;. </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty sure I get this Bias from some of the Thaumaturgy companions/Mage, where it's pretty explicit you need a belief system behind magic. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So says you, not the lore on the clan. This lore, instead, says that if something works, you must first accept that it works and shut the fuck up, your make belief won't stop it from working. If it works, it has some truth, if it has some truth, it can and should be studied by its own merits. <br /> <br /> <br /> You can understand the difference between &quot;studying several approaches to magic to <u><b>develop</b></u> new Paths and rituals&quot; and &quot;Having a <b><u>deep</u></b> understanding just to cast a spell&quot;. I was comparing <b>Dur-An-Ki</b> with frigging <b>Tremere Hermeticism</b>. One of them accepts that every magic that works, works as is. The other that every magic that works must be disassembled and understood under the light of their own unifying theory, to only then be reassembled as <b>Thaumaturgy</b>. Both have to study and understand what they do, but their requirements for mere replication aren't equal. </div> </div> </div>If everything just works as-is then the Assamites wouldn't be paying increased costs to learn non-assamite magic and they would have more abilities than the Tremere. Assamites need to adapt magic to their systems just as anyone else does. Also I think such an idealistic approach to magic, if that is indeed what LotC says, should be treated with suspicion. If this was the case, the Assamites would simply be better and more forward thinking than the Tremere, which isn't likely given the worldviews of the clans and how they work with others. It might make sense from a &quot;minorities (assamites) vs majority (Tremere)&quot; narrative but that's very simplistic.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Both politick and both have arcane power as a badge of status, but their internal economy is completely different for several reasons. Their caste system and few numbers pretty much force them to cooperate as a means to keep their status among the castes, their goal of breaking their clan-wide curse(s) presses them to work together for an answer to the conundrum, their less hierarchical internal structure (yes, they have a hierarchy, but you won't say that they have something even remotely similar to the Pyramid, will you?) means that to establish the pecking order and benefit from internal status they have to spread knowledge instead of hoard it (as in academia, you raise by being the most copied and referred, not by hoarding). </div> </div> </div>-forcing everyone to work to break the clan wide curse is dumb. Someone with thaumaturgy 5 and knowledges at 5 or less isn't going to be useful against a curse levied probaby at the 8+ range. <br /> -I fail to see your reasoning as to why the AsSc would be more cooperative than the Tremere. Do you think Regents don't want to see their chantries do well or something? Do you think AsSc will have more time to research and share given their messy structure? <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is taken directly from the entry on their magic from <i>Clanbook: Assamite</i> and this is the only Blood Magic with such an entry of a rule to learn magic from other traditions. If this RAW isn't RAI, I don't know what the hell it could be, and if an ST will allow or not this is not my problem. I would actually make it easier because I think having both higher XP cost and higher requirements in everything else is stupid, but YMMV. </div> </div> </div>Necromancy and Koldunic sorcery aren't &quot;Traditions&quot; they're completely different practices. Hell, necromancy explicitly flavours itself based on the culture of the ghosts you're messing with. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mage has a Library Background, but it doesn't have a similar effect (and Mage has this kind of effect from a lot of things). I do have a faint memory of seeing a Vampire book that had Library, but I can't quite remember which book. I'm almost sure it wasn't like the effect of <i>Kalif</i>. </div> </div> </div>I can't remember either, but I'm sure it's there... maybe it's there twice? I think I've read a couple different rules for Kalif, but it's been a while there too. <br /> There were some other things like &quot;sanctuary&quot; or something or such too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-09T12:46:50-07:00","id":1486109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486063#post1486063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't there a library background to make it easier with less sideaffects?</div> </div> </div> </div>Blood Magic: Secrets of Thaumaturgy has the Occult Library background which lowers the difficulty <i>and </i>adds dice to the pool on Occult rolls (p.89)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-09T14:14:59-07:00","id":1486113,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486109#post1486109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Blood Magic: Secrets of Thaumaturgy has the Occult Library background which lowers the difficulty <i>and </i>adds dice to the pool on Occult rolls (p.89)</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you, that was what I was thinking of<br /> (there might be another somewhere too)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Back on topic<br /> IMO, AsSc is the weakest of the big three styles of vampire thaumaturgy, but there are many minor styles of thaumaturgy that might be much more incomplete, limited, or costly. This may be something known like &quot;punk&quot; sorcery (small list of powers, dangerous practices) or something super niche (EG a delusional Malkavian/thinblood thinks he's an esper and can use movement of the mind and nothing else)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-09T17:17:26-07:00","id":1486126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pretty sure I get this Bias from some of the Thaumaturgy companions/Mage, where it's pretty explicit you need a belief system behind magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why I said this is a common trope in the setting. Mage definitely does this almost constantly and links it to lesser practices. But this is not Mage. The Tremere also talk a lot about this and this is frequently present in descriptions of Thaumaturgy, specially in the companions. But we're not talking Thaumaturgy, either.<br /> <br /> I think there are other situations where this is stated, it really is a very common notion in WoD that belief matters and magic is will-working. <b>But this doesn't apply to Dur-An-Ki</b>.<br /> <br /> Even when the Companions talk about the whole belief shenanigans in the <b>general</b> dissertations, this trope simply isn't there for this form of Blood Magic. And this <b>is</b> a trope, not a hard fact about magic, even for people irl that believe in magic.<br /> <br /> You either accept the clan's tropes on magic or houserule them, but this is their official lore.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If everything just works as-is then the Assamites wouldn't be paying increased costs to learn non-assamite magic and they would have more abilities than the Tremere.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, the rules are really bad in parsing the distinctions the lore puts out for them. I'd also say that with the current lore it makes little sense that most Blood Magic have access to such a limited list of Paths.<br /> <br /> The simple truth is the same as ever: the lore has some inconsistencies and the rules worsen them, and the Tremere <b>are</b> superior, but <i>part</i> of their superiority is simply the authors giving more tools for them than to the others because a) they're the main magical faction for play and b) are the personal favorites of Mark and probably a lot of the authors due to <i>Ars Magika</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">forcing everyone to work to break the clan wide curse is dumb. Someone with thaumaturgy 5 and knowledges at 5 or less isn't going to be useful against a curse levied probaby at the 8+ range.</div> </div> </div> </div>Lore vs rule, it is not a clash between two established powers and who has the better pool wins, it is an in-lore problem being solved through research and anyone can help with gathering data, throwing ideas or whatever.<br /> <br /> They develop the answer together, then the best caster casts it if that's the case. But this is not the kind of problem you should dismiss just based on mechanics, the problem itself has no mechanics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I fail to see your reasoning as to why the AsSc would be more cooperative than the Tremere. Do you think Regents don't want to see their chantries do well or something? Do you think AsSc will have more time to research and share given their messy structure?</div> </div> </div> </div>Did I say any of those things?<br /> <br /> I said it was easier to find a willing teacher for a given Path or Ritual among the Sorcerers, not that their collective effort was more cooperative or effective.<br /> <br /> The Tremere share with higher-ups while keeping peers in the dark to not be outmaneuvered. And their standardization certainly means that any collective effort is far more effective as they all speak the same magical language. What I said is that the Sorcerers have less reason to deny teaching to an equal, period.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Necromancy and Koldunic sorcery aren't &quot;Traditions&quot; they're completely different practices. Hell, necromancy explicitly flavours itself based on the culture of the ghosts you're messing with.</div> </div> </div> </div>From V20 Core:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>pp. 159, Necromancy, paragraph 1</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Necromancy is both a Discipline and a school of<br /> blood magic devoted to the command of the souls of the<br /> dead. It\u2019s similar to Thaumaturgy in that it has several<br /> \u201cpaths\u201d and accompanying \u201crituals\u201d rather than a strict<br /> linear progression of powers.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>pp. 448, Koldunic Sorcery, paragraph 1, bold by me</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The actual <b>casting</b> of Koldunic sorcery requires more<br /> than a clumsy exertion of will. Such <b>magic</b> demands<br /> perfection of form and mastery of the appropriate lore.<br /> The caster\u2019s player spends one blood point and rolls<br /> (Attribute) + Occult against a difficulty of the power\u2019s<br /> level + 3, with the specific Attribute listed for each<br /> path or \u201cway.\u201d Vampires always use the base Attribute,<br /> ignoring any bonuses gained from blood expenditure<br /> or other Disciplines. All kolduns must select one of the<br /> ways listed below <b>as their primary path</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>And the books that talk about Blood Magic in general also talk about those two Disciplines. In no place is it ever said that they're more &quot;not Traditions&quot; than other Blood Magics referred by their own Disciplines.<br /> <br /> Unless you read that rule as meaning that an user of <b>Dur-An-Ki</b> has to pay more to learn and use powers of other traditions <i>of <b>Dur-An-Ki</b></i>, this distinction of yours is merely mental gymnastics.<br /> <br /> Is it a valid distinction to apply on your own games? Sure, I think so, maybe would apply it myself. But it is not what the rule says, and I don't see any strong indication that it is not what the rule was intended to mean.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I've read a couple different rules for Kalif, but it's been a while there too.</div> </div> </div> </div>I saw it mentioned in <i>Libellus Sanguinis 3</i>, but don't remember any actual rule other than the one in <i>Rites of the Blood</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486108#post1486108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There were some other things like &quot;sanctuary&quot; or something or such too.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it gave any direct difficulty reduction for casting, though. I won't say fufrther because we're in the same e spot about this again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486109#post1486109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Blood Magic: Secrets of Thaumaturgy has the Occult Library background which lowers the difficulty <i>and </i>adds dice to the pool on Occult rolls (p.89)</div> </div> </div> </div>That wouldn't help Blood Magic, though, as it is chiefly rolled with Willpower, not Occult (the only exception in V20 being Koldunic Sorcery).<br /> <br /> For now <i>Kalif</i> keeps being the only source available to reduce difficulty for Blood Magic rolls in Vampire.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T02:04:28-07:00","id":1486142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486029#post1486029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Assamite Dur-An-Ki</b><br /> Scope: Like hermeticism, Dur-An-Ki, also encompases everything, but unlike hermetics, Assamite sorcers do not &amp;quot;meet in the middle&amp;quot;. Dur-an-Ki is too broad. It contains both dirty witches making covenants with spirits and elegant scholars drawing power from the stars: these people aren't likely to get along and see the opposite extreme's practices as bad for their own. I give it a 5/10.<br /> Mechanism: there's no standardization and not much in the way of shared beliefs. Also most books have such mages require drugs or pain or some other extreme state. 3/10.<br /> Structure: No standardization, being dependent/hostage to the other castes, not really having local Assamite sorcerers who can spend time to train neonates means an increased reliance on embracing the rare few who already practice magic, further limiting numbers and muddling the standardization 3/10.<br /> <br /> Average: 3.6. They're failures who rely on natural talent and who are pigeonholed into an undesirable system by an unforgiving caste system. But magic in the WoD isn't about talent, it's about knowledge. There might be some exceptional individuals among their ranks that can go toe to toe with some of the best Tremere has to offer, but the Tremere has more of those.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a minor thing, but your Scope rating here seems more related to organisational problems, so is already factored into the 3/10 given later for that criterion.<br /> <br /> If you focus solely on scope, I think 5/10 is a bit too punitive a score. I'd give it 6/10 or maybe even 7/10, because they can learn other paths, but the cost is more prohibitive. Then again, paths are too cheap anyway, so...<br /> <br /> I guess the issue shown in this thread is that Assamite Sorcery isn't always written consistently.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T04:47:27-07:00","id":1486145,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I think you're understating how Paradigm can be a problem even if we don't get into mage territory: <br /> Let me give a bad but easy-to-understand example. <br /> Let's say AsSc grows up in the middle east and is used to Greek notions of the Sun being male, the Moon being female. Let's say he knows a spell that draws from one of these figures and their associations with gender, maybe calling on Helios gives him glowing manly charm or whatever. <br /> He comes across a Telyav that shares with him an interesting spell related to femininity that draws upon the sun for it's feminity, because in baltic paganism/languages the sun's female and the moon is male. <br /> To work the Telyav's spell, the AsSc will need to unlearn what he knows, because fundamental pieces of information contradict eachother and in neither mythology does the sun change it's role. <br /> <br /> It would be less spicy to talk about planets (and less Ironic, since we all know vampires shouldn't really be calling on the sun for power): If in the Assamite's mind Mars is associated with war, how does he adopt a spell that associates the red planet with rivers, prosperity, tits and wine? When vamps are making their rituals, they aren't just drawing a specific picture in blood, everything has meanings to them that the vampire has internalized. How can a vampire internalize sets of polar opposite information? 1+2=3, but if sometimes 2 is actually 4 and so sometimes 1+2=5, how are you to perform simple equations involving the number 2? <br /> <br /> <br /> It's all well and good to say you can just &quot;perform the magic however you want because you only care about the effect rather than the method&quot; but if 2 is sometimes 4, 4 is sometimes 3, and 6 is sometimes 9 and 7 changes every day then how the hell are you going to organize anything? If you really could get the magic to work by just copying down the ritual's form without needing any of the beliefs behind it, it would take minutes to learn level 5 rituals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T05:37:50-07:00","id":1486146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486145#post1486145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I think you're understating how Paradigm can be a problem even if we don't get into mage territory: <br /> Let me give a bad but easy-to-understand example. <br /> Let's say AsSc grows up in the middle east and is used to Greek notions of the Sun being male, the Moon being female. Let's say he knows a spell that draws from one of these figures and their associations with gender, maybe calling on Helios gives him glowing manly charm or whatever. <br /> He comes across a Telyav that shares with him an interesting spell related to femininity that draws upon the sun for it's feminity, because in baltic paganism/languages the sun's female and the moon is male. <br /> To work the Telyav's spell, the AsSc will need to unlearn what he knows, because fundamental pieces of information contradict eachother and in neither mythology does the sun change it's role. <br /> <br /> It would be less spicy to talk about planets (and less Ironic, since we all know vampires shouldn't really be calling on the sun for power): If in the Assamite's mind Mars is associated with war, how does he adopt a spell that associates the red planet with rivers, prosperity, tits and wine? When vamps are making their rituals, they aren't just drawing a specific picture in blood, everything has meanings to them that the vampire has internalized. How can a vampire internalize sets of polar opposite information? 1+2=3, but if sometimes 2 is actually 4 and so sometimes 1+2=5, how are you to perform simple equations involving the number 2? <br /> <br /> <br /> It's all well and good to say you can just &quot;perform the magic however you want because you only care about the effect rather than the method&quot; but if 2 is sometimes 4, 4 is sometimes 3, and 6 is sometimes 9 and 7 changes every day then how the hell are you going to organize anything? If you really could get the magic to work by just copying down the ritual's form without needing any of the beliefs behind it, it would take minutes to learn level 5 rituals.</div> </div> </div> </div>In this example, though, you don't necessarily need to start seeing the sun or Mars as female. You just need to translate it into your own paradigm. So maybe the Assamite Sorcerer calls on the moon or Venus instead of the sun or Mars. In his view, the spell might work for the Telyav not because the opposing paradigm is right; it's just that the Telyav has hit on the right combination of things to get it to work, regardless of invoking the wrong being. <br /> <br /> And we do see from some of the renamed Paths that Assamites do this -- they attribute powers to whichever god makes sense to them. As do other paradigms.<br /> <br /> In reality, this isn't an insurmountable issue. For the player, the main penalty is the extra XP cost. But provided they can come up with a suitable way to import the path, they can certainly pull it off once they have the XP needed.<br /> <br /> In my view, paradigm is a bigger problem when it comes to how you *cast* magic. Because getting stoned is not as quick or reliable as spending a point of something. The Assamite who needs to get high or achieve ecstasy requires a lot more time to do the same thing. That's, for me, the main downside.<br /> <br /> But you may be right that the scope element is a 5/10, because that combination of increased XP and slower casting is definitely a barrier. I'm still leaning towards 6/10 myself, because it's not half as useful as the Tremere paradigm, but I agree it's the worst of the three.<br /> <br /> Now, worse than all three of these is Koldunic Sorcery, IMO. It's very limited, carries a high price by implication (you're binding yourself to Kupala or similar spirits) and it's probably rarer than both Tremere and Assamite Sorcery.<br /> <br /> Abyssal Mysticism might be even narrower, though it keys off Obtenebration, so that makes up for it (Obtenebration is powerful without without rituals).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T08:16:45-07:00","id":1486152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486146#post1486146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Now, worse than all three of these is Koldunic Sorcery, IMO. It's very limited, carries a high price by implication (you're binding yourself to Kupala or similar spirits) and it's probably rarer than both Tremere and Assamite Sorcery.<br /> <br /> Abyssal Mysticism might be even narrower, though it keys off Obtenebration, so that makes up for it (Obtenebration is powerful without without rituals).</div> </div> </div> </div>Koldunic sorcery has the most terrible name, it's means &quot;Wizard Sorcery&quot; in Russian and almost &quot;Dumpling Sorcery&quot; in Lithuanian.<br /> But I think it's clear that although it's not as versatile as thaumaturgy, the paths are much more powerful.<br /> (Some of them are also more bottom heavy, like the Way of Fire 2 is absurdly strong while 3+ isn't really so useful)<br /> Kraina, however, suck.<br /> It's not thaumaturgy; It's something else. I'd rationalize it as a Discipline that offers spirit control or... something. IE you're not using your will to bend the world, you're using spirits to bend the world. I think one of they key things is that there's actually a few passives in Koldunic sorcery (Way of fire 1, IDK what else there is) whilst all other thaumaturgy and necromancy has had active abilities only.<br /> Still, it's explicitly a power you can only ever learn at out of Clan rates (I'm not sure if such a rule applies to Caitiff)<br /> <br /> <br /> Abyss Mysticism is either absurdly overpowered (or at least cost effective) or a joke, depending on what supplement you're reading.<br /> Personally, I like &quot;Severe but temporary sideaffects&quot;. I'd be open to paying slightly more XP to have abyss mysticism without sideffects. Vampires shouldn't have permanent appearance changes (beyond Vicissitude) or gain permanent flaws so easily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T08:47:37-07:00","id":1486154,"title":"","content":"I think arguing about the relative merits or shortcomings of different styles of blood magic is, ultimately, missing the point.<br /> <br /> The reason Assamite Sorcerers got the short end of the stick, in terms of Rituals, is because the writers failed to write ones that compared favorably to that of other styles.<br /> <br /> It's not a Watsonian issue. It's a Doylist issue.<br /> <br /> As for WHY the writers gave such poor support for Assamites, I imagine it's because they reserved their best material for the Tremere. Unless the Rituals were specifically built off of a Clan's particular esoterica or mythology, or which specifically served that Clan's purposes, the writers devoted their attention to the Tremere, the designated Protagonist blood sorcerers. (&quot;Designated Protagonist&quot; by virtue of being the first blood sorcerers invented for the game, and the ones most considered &quot;vanilla&quot; for VtM's purposes).<br /> <br /> <br /> For that matter, I'd like to deconstruct the concept of Tremere Hermeticism as being uniquely capable of adapting the magic of others.<br /> <br /> While this has some metatextual significance, because Real Life Hermeticism (especially Golden Dawn Hermeticism) was a mish-mash of esoteric ideas cobbled together into a larger framework, there's no real basis for assuming Hermeticism is INHERENTLY good at adapting. History just happened to shake out that way, as Hermeticism and related fields like Alchemy and Astrology were &quot;permitted&quot; in post-Christian Europe in a way other magical practices were not. In another, hypothetical reality, other cultural traditions of mysticism may have filled the role of &quot;esoteric refuge&quot;, by dint of esoteric imperialism and systemization.<br /> <br /> Indeed, we see a bit of this in Assamite Sorcery and Setite Sorcery. Melting pots of esoteric ideas and magical practices from different cultures. They just treated them more as Art or Religion, while the Tremere used their workings more as Science. (But I contend that ALL Magical Practice, regardless of tradition, is a mixture of Art, Craft, Religion, and Science, just to varying degrees).<br /> <br /> But I digress.<br /> <br /> My point is we only really have the word of old White Wolf's writers that the Tremere were uniquely good at adapting esoteric practice. Or rather, they only became so because the writers made it so. We can make all kinds of explanations for how it worked in-universe. But ultimately, it was an authorial decision to lend more weight to the Tremere. And they did it because they had established very early in VtM's lifespan that the Tremere were the top dog blood sorcerers. Metatextual parallels to Real World esoteric history aside, we have the Tremere we have because White Wolf already decided the Tremere were Just That Good, and needed to backport lore to justify it.<br /> <br /> Same as why a House of wizards-turned-vampires managed to survive making enemies with half the vampiric world and their fellow wizards. They can't have succumbed to any of those enemies, or they wouldn't be a pillar of the dominant vampiric world order in the modern day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T11:37:39-07:00","id":1486164,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a><br /> The <i>Foundations</i> of Hermeticism are built upon the idea of adaptation: All religions are partly true and partly false, let's approach everything with an open mind and adapt what we think works well with other ideas and maybe not think too much about the very specific issues that cause sectarian strife. A Jew, A Hindu and a Daoist can all walk into a bar and... discuss the deeper nature of the universe.<br /> <br /> One of the most frustrating things about how Hermeticism is treated by WW is in the confusion of it's place: By being progressive, open and inviting on a philosophical level (relative to magic practices), it becomes the natural option for dominance. By being dominant, it becomes, or is seen to become, conservative, closed and exclusive on a political level; As a result, writers imagine that it is likewise conservative, closed and exclusive on a philosophical level.<br /> <br /> To use an example more relevant to today:<br /> Jesus was a very nice guy with progressive values=&gt;followers grow in number=&gt;Followers gain power=&gt;followers abuse power and become pricks=&gt;people think Christianity is a nasty religion conservatives use to justify conservative bullshit.<br /> <br /> There's an inverse to this<br /> Someone makes an exclusive or otherwise problematic belief system=&gt;belief gets smaller=&gt;belief becomes a minority that stands outside the mainstream=&gt;Outsiders support the belief because they like an underdog and like challenges to the mainstream, outsiders can willfully ignore how bigoted this group is because they're a minority. .<br /> <br /> <br /> Tremere Hermeticism -works- because it adapts well. It's flexible, it's robust, it's broadly applicable. Then of course it dominated, and now it's &quot;The Man&quot; thaumaturgy and is seen as rigidly conservative, while something genuinely conservative like &quot;Old Skool&quot; thaumaturgy is the product of plucky anarchs. Similarly, Assamites formed a majority-reactionary sect but because they're the &quot;underdog minority&quot; compared to the Camarilla they get more liberal flags than they really should.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 07-10-2022, 02:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-10T12:03:13-07:00","id":1486168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486145#post1486145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I think you're understating how Paradigm can be a problem even if we don't get into mage territory:<br /> Let me give a bad but easy-to-understand example.<br /> Let's say AsSc grows up in the middle east and is used to Greek notions of the Sun being male, the Moon being female. Let's say he knows a spell that draws from one of these figures and their associations with gender, maybe calling on Helios gives him glowing manly charm or whatever.<br /> He comes across a Telyav that shares with him an interesting spell related to femininity that draws upon the sun for it's feminity, because in baltic paganism/languages the sun's female and the moon is male.<br /> To work the Telyav's spell, the AsSc will need to unlearn what he knows, because fundamental pieces of information contradict eachother and in neither mythology does the sun change it's role.</div> </div> </div> </div>The simple answer to that conundrum is that a Sorcerer can study and understand several different ways of thinking about something and compartmentalize to work with this or that, as much as later examine their differences and similarities to draw conclusions without having to assume they're reaching a unifying view, just examining two aspects of reality.<br /> <br /> And that is <b>exactly</b> how a lot of thought works IRL. Scientists are perfectly able to know conflicting theories in their fields and examining a given phenomena or a colleague's work in the light of one or another regardless of personal belief. Philosophers content with conflicting ideas all the time and how different worldviews would parse something. Anthropologists, Historians and Archaeologists have an understanding of how distinct cultures would explain a given phenomena at different times, and even which kinds of divergent thought exists within the group. Anyone who speaks more than one language learns to change between very distinct codes for understanding and parsing the world, a difference so deep that people can notice changes in personality as they switch codes.<br /> <br /> Oh, and we as RPG players can understand all those paradigms from different splats, plus different games, and switch to a single one with ease to roleplay a character, delve deeply into it and develop it, and still be able to come out and switch again to another.<br /> <br /> So, no, I'm not understating it. This is a trope. Reality has plenty of deep, complex paradigms with real applications that people has to navigate and we do just fine. Anything you can say about how magic would be an exception isn't a fact, just a trope. The Banu Haqim do not ascribe to this trope and that's all. The answer is simply that when the Sorcerer will perform a ritual devised in another paradigm they'll follow that other paradigm, that they also understand to an enough degree.since they studied it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486146#post1486146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Abyssal Mysticism might be even narrower, though it keys off Obtenebration, so that makes up for it (Obtenebration is powerful without without rituals).</div> </div> </div> </div>The combination of XP cost for each and every single ritual, steep drawbacks and permanent transformations associated with each one and a very narrow (if powerful) scope of powers just makes Abyss Mysticism the worse in comparison with other Blood Magics. What is made stronger by its connection to Obtenebration isn't AM as Blood Magic, but Obt as a Discipline. Even then, not by much.<br /> <br /> But you know what would be really interesting? To expand the notion of Abyss Mysticism to other Disciplines, so we can have advanced powers for them at an XP cost and at lower levels than 6, incorporating the notion that every Discipline is a broader manifestation of the Blood than meets the eye at first. Imagine how Vampire would be with such a concept? We only need a name to describe this kind of powers, something that highlights how much the Kindred study that Discipline with dedication and <b>Devotion</b>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486152#post1486152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Koldunic sorcery has the most terrible name, it's means &quot;Wizard Sorcery&quot; in Russian and almost &quot;Dumpling Sorcery&quot; in Lithuanian.<br /> But I think it's clear that although it's not as versatile as thaumaturgy, the paths are much more powerful.<br /> (Some of them are also more bottom heavy, like the Way of Fire 2 is absurdly strong while 3+ isn't really so useful)<br /> Kraina, however, suck.<br /> It's not thaumaturgy; It's something else. I'd rationalize it as a Discipline that offers spirit control or... something. IE you're not using your will to bend the world, you're using spirits to bend the world. I think one of they key things is that there's actually a few passives in Koldunic sorcery (Way of fire 1, IDK what else there is) whilst all other thaumaturgy and necromancy has had active abilities only.<br /> Still, it's explicitly a power you can only ever learn at out of Clan rates (I'm not sure if such a rule applies to Caitiff)<br /> <br /> <br /> Abyss Mysticism is either absurdly overpowered (or at least cost effective) or a joke, depending on what supplement you're reading.<br /> Personally, I like &quot;Severe but temporary sideaffects&quot;. I'd be open to paying slightly more XP to have abyss mysticism without sideffects. Vampires shouldn't have permanent appearance changes (beyond Vicissitude) or gain permanent flaws so easily.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here I mostly agree with everything. They could have just called it Koldovstvo, would be kinda weird, but less stupid.<br /> <br /> But yeah, while the rules don't make a big difference between Ways and Paths, the theme is clearly of something fundamentally distinct and more shamanistic in nature, and the Ways work to make it stand alone even if limited in scope.<br /> <br /> The Krainas I think are a good idea with a bad execution, but could be worked upon. A Koldun that derives specific magic from the land is very fitting and an interesting flavor, but they could and should just ditch their Highlander Feelings about the matter.<br /> <br /> Abyss Mysticism is the worse simply because of those damned permanent side effects. That's simply bad design, a very stupid idea that makes almost every power a bad choice unless it fits your character concept in a very narrow way. I know the Abyss Mystics are meant to be really weird and set apart, but that's just ridiculous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1486014-is-assamite-sorcery-the-weakest-blood-magic-tradition?p=1486154#post1486154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For that matter, I'd like to deconstruct the concept of Tremere Hermeticism as being uniquely capable of adapting the magic of others.</div> </div> </div> </div>Regardless of agreeing or not to MWIS's views on Hermeticism (by the way, <i>modern</i> Hermeticism), it is the native idea of the setting.<br /> <br /> Your deconstruction is valid on itself, but it ignores the assumptions and intentions of the authors and the history of the setting itself.<br /> <br /> <i>Ars Magika</i> was created around Hermeticism, it is a heavy part of it, and it is the primary influence for WoD in general. The Tremere come from there, Thaumaturgy come from there, so they're basically the first foot of the Order of Hermes in the setting from a Doylist perspective.<br /> <br /> That's why so many places in the setting ascribe in some degree (and usually a big degree) to a Hermetic paradigm, and this is no less true for Vampire even if it is less intrinsic to it.<br /> <br /> In this, we're talking about modern Hermeticism as defined in Thelema and the Golden Dawn, and this school of occult though is the very one that ascribes magic to a work of Will over the universe, where magic comes from imposing the magician's will over reality and paradigms work as a focus lens for that will.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. Most of the setting was built with this idea in mind and the Tremere didn't get the lion's share of Blood Magic just because they're &quot;vanilla&quot;, but actually because they're author favorites that were built in VtM to have this lion's share.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}